## Context

Celebrate sweep after merging the OpenAI key path fix found multiple instances
of credential loading patterns across the codebase:
- direct reads from `~/.config/keys/*.env`
- provider-specific fallback logic duplicated in scripts and adapters
- mixed variable contracts (`*_AEDIST` vs canonical env names)
- inject from project .env

This indicates a class-shaped regression risk: key-loading drift can return in
unrelated changes and silently break one provider path.

## Actions

1. Define a canonical secret-loading policy for AEDIST scripts/adapters
   - Source of truth is ~/.config.env only
   - Only the run-with-key wrapper access it
   - All other scripts read from their environment
   - Fallback behavior is fail early with a clear warning
   - No hardcoding keys in source ever !!!
   - No fallback search in other locations
   - Use only project keys: uniform env var names to *_API_KEY_AEDIST for api keys.
2. Add an adherence check in tests to detect policy drift mechanically.
3. Wire the adherence check into fast verification (`make check-fast` if feasible).
4. Document exceptions explicitly if some adapters must keep file fallbacks.

## First test

Add a grep-backed adherence test that fails when new direct key-file reads or
non-canonical key-variable patterns are introduced outside approved modules.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] Policy for secret loading is documented and repository-scoped
- [ ] Regression test exists and fails on a seeded policy-violation fixture
- [ ] Regression test passes on current code after approved exceptions are marked
- [ ] Test is included in routine local verification path
- [ ] Follow-up tickets are split if implementation exceeds one merge request
